= 1
{parent=2010/A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=1}

For $N=\lfloor x\rfloor$, interchange a finite sum and an integral:
$$
\int_1^x S(t)F'(t)\,dt
=\sum_{n\le x}f(n)\int_n^x F'(t)\,dt
=F(x)S(x)-\sum_{n\le x}f(n)F(n).
$$
This proves the <Abel's summation formula>, including the term at $n=1$.

Apply it with $f(n)=1$, $F(t)=1/t$. Writing $\{t\}=t-\lfloor t\rfloor$ gives
$$
H_{\lfloor x\rfloor}=\log x+1-\frac{\{x\}}x-\int_1^x\frac{\{t\}}{t^2}\,dt
=\log x+\gamma-\frac{\{x\}}x+\int_x^\infty\frac{\{t\}}{t^2}\,dt.
$$
The last two terms are $O(x^{-1})$, proving the first expansion. For the refinement let $B_1(t)=\{t\}-1/2$. It has mean <zero> on each unit interval, so its <primitive> $P(t)=\int_0^tB_1(u)\,du$ is bounded and periodic. <Integration by parts> gives
$$
\int_x^\infty\frac{B_1(t)}{t^2}\,dt
=-\frac{P(x)}{x^2}+2\int_x^\infty\frac{P(t)}{t^3}\,dt=O(x^{-2}).
$$
Thus for <integer> $x$, where $\{x\}=0$,
$$
\boxed{H_x=\log x+\gamma+\frac1{2x}+O(x^{-2}).}
$$
For the zeta representation, integrating each defining integral by parts yields
$$
sf_n(s)=-(n+1)^{-s}+\frac{(n+1)^{1-s}-n^{1-s}}{1-s}.
$$
Summing from $1$ to $N-1$ and substituting into the definition of $\zeta$ gives
$$
\boxed{\zeta(s)=\frac{N^{1-s}}{s-1}+\sum_{n\le N}n^{-s}-s\sum_{n=N}^\infty f_n(s).}
$$
Since $0\le x-n\le1$,
$$
\left|\sum_{n=N}^\infty f_n(s)\right|
\le\sum_{n=N}^\infty\int_n^{n+1}x^{-\sigma-1}\,dx
=\frac{N^{-\sigma}}\sigma.
$$
This is uniform on compact subsets of $\operatorname{Re}s>0$, so the series is <holomorphic> there and limits at $s=1$ are legitimate after subtracting the <pole>. Expanding $N^{1-s}=1-(s-1)\log N+O((s-1)^2)$ gives
$$
a_0=H_N-\log N-\sum_{n=N}^\infty f_n(1).
$$
The final tail is $O(N^{-1})$, and the harmonic expansion sends the remaining difference to $\gamma$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{a_0=\gamma.}
$$

= 2
{parent=2010/A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

Use the convention $f(1)=1$ in the definition of a <multiplicative arithmetic function>. For a finite set of primes, multiplying the <absolutely convergent> local series gives the Dirichlet sum over integers supported on those primes, by <unique prime factorization>. If all primes up to $Y$ are included, any missing <integer> is larger than $Y$, so the difference from the full series is bounded by $\sum_{n>Y}|f(n)|n^{-\sigma}$, which tends to <zero>. This proves the <Euler product> in its absolute-convergence half-plane.

For coprime $m,n$, every divisor of $mn$ is uniquely a product of a divisor of $m$ and a divisor of $n$. Hence $\sigma_\alpha(mn)=\sigma_\alpha(m)\sigma_\alpha(n)$. The squared modulus is also multiplicative when $\alpha=i\theta$.

At a prime put $z=p^{-s}$ and $a=p^{i\theta}$, so $|a|=1$. Summing by the two divisor exponents gives
$$
\sum_{k\ge0}\left|\sum_{j=0}^ka^j\right|^2z^k
=\frac{1-z^2}{(1-z)^2(1-az)(1-a^{-1}z)}.
$$
For example, expand the square as $\sum_{j,l\le k}a^{j-l}$ and sum $z^k$ from $k=\max(j,l)$ to obtain this rational expression. It remains valid for $a=1$. <Absolute convergence> for $\sigma>1$ follows from $|\sigma_{i\theta}(p^k)|\le k+1$ and convergence of $\sum_p p^{-\sigma}$. Multiplying the factors therefore proves
$$
\boxed{\sum_{n\ge1}\frac{|\sigma_{i\theta}(n)|^2}{n^s}
=\frac{\zeta(s)^2\zeta(s+i\theta)\zeta(s-i\theta)}{\zeta(2s)}.}
$$
Suppose now that $\zeta(1+i\theta)=0$. Necessarily $\theta\ne0$, since $s=1$ is a <pole>. <Complex conjugation> gives a <zero> also at $1-i\theta$. In the numerator, the <double pole> at $s=1$ is canceled by those two zeros in the shifted factors. At $s=1\pm i\theta$, the possible <simple pole> of a shifted factor is canceled by the double <zero> of $\zeta(s)^2$. Thus on $\operatorname{Re}s>0$ the only possible remaining poles come from zeros of $\zeta(2s)$. Since the <Euler product> excludes zeros for $\operatorname{Re}(2s)>1$, these possible poles lie in $0<\operatorname{Re}s\le1/2$. In particular $f$ is regular for $\operatorname{Re}s>1/2$.

The assumed nonnegative-series principle now makes its <Dirichlet series> converge and equal $f$ there. Its first coefficient is one and all coefficients are nonnegative, so $f(\sigma)\ge1$ for real $\sigma>1/2$. But as $\sigma\downarrow1/2$, the numerator remains finite while $\zeta(2\sigma)\to+\infty$, forcing $f(\sigma)\to0$. This contradiction proves
$$
\boxed{\zeta(s)\text{ has no zero on }\operatorname{Re}s=1.}
$$
At the real point $s=1$ the statement is understood as a <pole> rather than a finite nonzero value. The <pole>-location argument did not assume the <zero>-free boundary before proving it.

= 3
{parent=2010/A}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

The vertical integral is <absolutely convergent> because the denominator is quadratic in the imaginary part. If $x<1$, move its line to the right: no poles are crossed, and the integral on $\operatorname{Re}s=R$ is $O(x^R/R)$, tending to <zero>. If $x>1$, move left across the simple poles at $0,-1$. Their residues are $1$ and $-x^{-1}$, and the far-left integral tends to <zero>. Horizontal segments vanish by their $O(|\operatorname{Im}s|^{-2})$ decay for a fixed pair of vertical lines. At $x=1$, dominated convergence on the original line gives the limiting value <zero>. Hence the <kernel> is $0$ below one and $1-x^{-1}$ at or above one.

On a line $c>2$, <absolute convergence> permits interchanging the totient <Dirichlet series> and this integral. The $n$th term is $x\phi(n)$ times the <kernel> at $x/n$, giving exactly $\phi(n)(x-n)$ for $n\le x$. Thus
$$
S(x):=\sum_{n\le x}\phi(n)(x-n)
=\frac1{2\pi i}\int_{c-i\infty}^{c+i\infty}
\frac{\zeta(s-1)}{\zeta(s)}\frac{x^{s+1}}{s(s+1)}\,ds.
$$
We may take $c=5/2$ and shift to $\operatorname{Re}s=3/2$. The denominator has no zeros in this strip; the only <pole> crossed is $s=2$, where $\zeta(s-1)$ has <residue> one. Its <residue> in the integrand is
$$
\frac{x^3}{6\zeta(2)}=\frac{x^3}{\pi^2}.
$$
On the new line, the supplied estimates bound the integrand by $O(x^{5/2}|t|^{-3/2}\log^8|t|)$ for $|t|\ge2$, an integrable function. The compact portion is bounded as well. The same estimates, uniformly across the strip, make the horizontal integrals tend to <zero>. Consequently
$$
\boxed{S(x)=\frac{x^3}{\pi^2}+O(x^{5/2})\sim\frac{x^3}{\pi^2}.}
$$
For the Tauberian step let $A(x)=\sum_{n\le x}\phi(n)$. It is nondecreasing because the summands are nonnegative, and $S(x)=\int_0^xA(t)\,dt$. For fixed $0<\epsilon<1$,
$$
\frac{S(x)-S((1-\epsilon)x)}{\epsilon x}
\le A(x)\le
\frac{S((1+\epsilon)x)-S(x)}{\epsilon x}.
$$
After division by $x^2$ and passage to lower and upper limits, the asymptotic for $S$ gives bounds $(3-3\epsilon+\epsilon^2)/\pi^2$ and $(3+3\epsilon+\epsilon^2)/\pi^2$. Letting $\epsilon\downarrow0$ squeezes them together. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\sum_{n\le x}\phi(n)\sim\frac{3x^2}{\pi^2}.}
$$
This uses monotonicity of the summatory function; differentiating an asymptotic formula without such control would not justify the conclusion.

