{title2=Elliptic curves}

= 4
{parent=2010/B}
{scope}

The <integer> $n=-3$ gives $n^3-5=-32$, whose 2-adic norm is $2^{-5}<2^{-3}$. For the 5-adic condition, if $5\nmid n$ then $n^3-5$ is a unit, while if $5\mid n$ its valuation is exactly one. Neither case has norm below $5^{-3}$. The Newton construction for the remaining requests follows.

= i
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Let $d=|f'(a_0)|>0$ and $q_0=|f(a_0)|/d^2<1$. At any step before reaching a root, set
$$
a_{i+1}=a_i+h_i,\qquad h_i=-\frac{f(a_i)}{f'(a_i)}.
$$
Assuming inductively that $|f'(a_i)|=d$ and $q_i=|f(a_i)|/d^2<1$, this gives
$$
\boxed{|a_{i+1}-a_i|=\frac{|f(a_i)|}{|f'(a_i)|}=dq_i.}
$$
All derivatives of a <polynomial> over $R$ are integral on $R$, so $d\le1$. Hence $|h_i|<1$, and the <ultrametric inequality> keeps $a_{i+1}$ in $R$. If a root is reached, retain that value for subsequent terms, and the asserted weak step inequality still holds.

= ii
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

<Taylor expansion> of $f'$ about $a_i$ has integral coefficients, so
$$
|f'(a_i+h_i)-f'(a_i)|\le|h_i|=dq_i<d.
$$
In a <non-Archimedean absolute value>, adding an error of norm strictly smaller than $d$ cannot change a quantity of norm $d$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{|f'(a_i)|=d\quad\text{for every }i.}
$$
This simultaneously verifies the <derivative> assumption used at the next step of part (i).

= iii
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The constant and linear Taylor terms cancel in the Newton step, leaving
$$
|f(a_{i+1})|\le|h_i|^2=d^2q_i^2,\qquad
q_{i+1}\le q_i^2\le q_0^{2^{i+1}}.
$$
Thus residuals strictly decrease whenever the current residual is nonzero, and the steps tend to <zero> doubly exponentially. By the <ultrametric inequality>, the distance between any two sufficiently late iterates is bounded by the largest intervening step. The sequence is Cauchy and converges in the complete <valuation ring> to $a$. Continuity gives $f(a)=0$, and
$$
\boxed{|a-a_0|\le dq_0=\frac{|f(a_0)|}{|f'(a_0)|}.}
$$
The literal infinite chain of strict inequalities needs the root-hit exception: if $f(a_0)=0$, or a later iterate is already a root, it cannot keep strictly decreasing below <zero>. The construction is eventually constant in that case; all existence and distance conclusions remain valid.

For the equality case, suppose $|f(a_0)|=d^2$ with $0<d<1$. The first Newton step has norm $|h_0|=d$. Write the <Taylor coefficient> of $h^2$ as $c_2(a_0)=f''(a_0)/2$. Its norm is strictly below one by hypothesis; all higher coefficients have norm at most one. Hence
$$
|f(a_0+h_0)|\le\max(|c_2(a_0)|d^2,d^3)<d^2.
$$
The <derivative> changes by $2c_2(a_0)h_0+O(h_0^2)$, of norm strictly below $d$, so it still has norm $d$. The strict Hensel argument applies from this first iterate onward, and the root remains within distance $d=|f(a_0)|/d$ of $a_0$. If characteristic two is admitted, interpret the displayed half-second-<derivative> as the second <Taylor coefficient> $\sum_j\binom j2c_jb^{j-2}$; this is the division-free form of the required hypothesis.

For the final local equation put $F(t)=t^3+3t$. Its values modulo nine are $0,4,5$, so the sum of two such values is confined to $0,1,4,5,8$. Conversely choose initial pairs for these five classes as
$$
(0,0),\quad(-1,-1),\quad(1,0),\quad(-1,0),\quad(1,1),
$$
respectively. Fix the second coordinate $y_0$ and apply the equality-case Hensel result to $g(X)=F(X)+F(y_0)-n$ at the first coordinate $x_0$. Its residual is divisible by nine, $g'(x_0)=3(x_0^2+1)$ has 3-adic norm $1/3$, and $g''(b)/2=3b$ has norm below one for every $b\in\mathbb Z_3$. The strict or equality case therefore supplies a root in $\mathbb Z_3$. Thus
$$
\boxed{n\equiv0,1,4,5,8\pmod9}
$$
is the necessary and sufficient condition.

= 5
{parent=2010/B}
{scope}

We first prove all three general torsion assertions before treating the lettered cases. If a finite point has $|x|_p>1$, the cubic term dominates the integral lower terms, so $v_p(x)=-2m$, $v_p(y)=-3m$ for an <integer> $m\ge1$. Its parameter $z=-x/y$ then lies in $p\mathbb Z_p$, with norm at most $p^{-1}$. The integral Weierstrass model defines its <formal group> at infinity over $\mathbb Z_p$: using $w=-1/y$ gives $w=z^3+Azw^2+Bw^3$, an integral power series starting in degree three.

The supplied torsion estimate excludes a nonzero torsion parameter for $p\ge3$, since $p^{-1}<p^{-1/(p-1)}$, and the lower bound increases for higher $p$-powers. For $p=2$ it excludes orders $2^n$ with $n\ge2$, because their bound is strictly above $1/2$. The remaining possibility is order two, which would imply the finite point equals its negative and hence $y=0$. A nonintegral $x$ cannot solve the monic cubic with integral coefficients, so this is impossible too. Thus every finite torsion point has integral $x$, and the equation then forces integral $y$.

For <good reduction> the reduction map is a <group homomorphism> whose <kernel> is this <formal group> at infinity. Its torsion-free <kernel> proves injectivity on torsion, giving a <subgroup> of the finite reduced group. Applying local integrality to a rational torsion point at every prime gives $x,y\in\mathbb Z$.

If $y\ne0$, the double of a rational torsion point is finite and integral. The doubling formula
$$
x(2P)=\frac{(3x^2+a)^2}{4y^2}-2x
$$
therefore implies $y^2\mid(3x^2+a)^2$. Also $y^2=x^3+ax+b$. Evaluating the supplied <polynomial> identity at the integral $x$ now gives
$$
\boxed{y=0\quad\text{or}\quad y^2\mid4a^3+27b^2.}
$$
This establishes the needed <Nagell–Lutz theorem> conclusions without assuming them in the lettered applications.

= a
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Nonsingularity with $b=a^2$ forces $a\ne0$. Take
$$
\boxed{P=(0,a).}
$$
It lies on the curve. Its <tangent> slope is $a/(2a)=1/2$, so the doubling formula gives $x(2P)=1/4$. If $P$ were torsion, $2P$ would be torsion as well, contradicting the integrality just proved. Therefore $P$ has infinite order.

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For $y^2=x^3+x+2$, the point $P=(1,2)$ doubles to $(-1,0)$: its <tangent> slope is $(3+1)/(2\cdot2)=1$, giving $x(2P)=-1$ and $y(2P)=0$. Thus $P$ has order four.

The curve has <good reduction> at $3$, since $4+27\cdot4=112$ is nonzero modulo three. Its reduced points are the point at infinity, two points above $x=1$, and one above $x=2$, so there are four in total. Injectivity of torsion reduction bounds the rational torsion order by four. The known four points exhaust it:
$$
\boxed{E(\mathbb Q)_{\rm tors}=\{O,(-1,0),(1,2),(1,-2)\}\cong C_4.}
$$

= c
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Modulo five the curve is $y^2=x^3+x$, with <good reduction> because $4a^3+27b^2\equiv4\pmod5$. It has one point above each of $x=0,2,3$ and no others, plus infinity, so its group has order four. Rational torsion therefore has order dividing four.

A nonzero <rational point> of order two would have $y=0$ and integral $x$, so would give an <integer> root of $x^3+ax+b$. But modulo two this <polynomial> is $x^3+x+1$, which is one at both residues. Thus there is no rational <2-torsion>. Every nontrivial finite group of order dividing four contains an element of order two, so
$$
\boxed{E(\mathbb Q)_{\rm tors}=\{O\}.}
$$

= 6
{parent=2010/B}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=6}

Complete the square-class map at the identity by $q(O)=1$; at the finite point $T=(0,0)$ its value is the class of $b$. For another <rational point> write
$$
x=r/s^2,\qquad y=t/s^3,\qquad\gcd(r,s)=1.
$$
These denominator powers follow because a negative valuation of $x$ forces the leading cubic term to dominate, so that valuation is even and the valuation of $y$ is three halves of it. The equation gives
$$
t^2=r(r^2+ars^2+bs^4).
$$
If a prime $\ell\nmid b$ divides $r$, it does not divide the second factor, since $\ell\nmid s$. Thus $v_\ell(r)$ is even. All denominator valuations are even too. Every image class therefore has a representative which is a signed squarefree divisor of $b$, proving that $\operatorname{im}q$ is finite.

Use the standard 2-isogeny with <kernel> $\{O,T\}$ and <dual isogeny> satisfying $\widehat\phi\phi=[2]$, $\phi\widehat\phi=[2]$. Away from the exceptional points one may take
$$
\phi(x,y)=\left(x+a+\frac bx,\ y\left(1-\frac b{x^2}\right)\right).
$$
Applying the analogous isogeny to $E'$ gives a curve with coefficients $4a,16b$; scaling its coordinates by $1/4,1/8$ gives the dual map back to $E$. The analogous descent map $q'$ on $D$ has <kernel> $\phi(C)$ and finite image. The sequence
$$
D/\phi(C)\ \xrightarrow{\widehat\phi}\ C/2C\longrightarrow C/\widehat\phi(D)\longrightarrow0
$$
is exact at the last two terms. Its outside groups are finite, identified with the two finite descent images, so **$C/2C$ is finite**.

For $E_p:y^2=x(x^2-p^2)$, the rational <2-torsion> is $O,(0,0),(p,0),(-p,0)$, of order four. The first descent image is exactly $\{1,-1,p,-p\}$: these classes occur on the <2-torsion>, and the divisor-support bound permits no others. The isogenous curve is $E'_p:y^2=x(x^2+4p^2)$. Its real points have $x\ge0$, so the second image lies in the four positive classes $\{1,2,p,2p\}$.

Here the <kernel> $\{O,(0,0)\}$ of the dual is contained in $\phi(C)$, since $\phi(\pm p,0)=(0,0)$. Thus the displayed map $D/\phi(C)\to C/2C$ is injective, and exactness gives
$$
|C/2C|=|\operatorname{im}q|\,|\operatorname{im}q'|=4|\operatorname{im}q'|.
$$
The allowed rank formula makes the left side $2^{R+2}$, so
$$
\boxed{2^R=|\operatorname{im}q'|\le4,\qquad R\le2.}
$$
Now consider the quartic relation. Normalize a nontrivial <integer> solution so that $p$ does not divide both $x,y$, by dividing them by $p$ and $z$ by $p^2$ whenever necessary. If $p\nmid x$, reduction modulo $p$ gives $x^4=2z^2$, with $z\ne0$ modulo $p$, making $2$ a <quadratic residue>. If $p\mid x$, then $p\nmid y$ and $p\mid z$; write $x=pX$, $z=pZ$, divide by $p^2$, and reduce to get $y^4=2Z^2$ modulo $p$. Again $2$ is a square. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\left(\frac2p\right)=1,\qquad p\equiv\pm1\pmod8.}
$$
If the class $2$ occurred in $\operatorname{im}q'$, write the corresponding coordinate as $x'=2u^2/v^2$. The curve equation then gives
$$
u^4+p^2v^4=2z^2,\qquad z=\frac{y'v^3}{4u}.
$$
Here $u,v$ are nonzero coprime integers and $z$ is rational. Since $2z^2$ is an <integer>, its reduced denominator must be one, so $z$ is an <integer> too. For $p\equiv3,5\pmod8$ this contradicts the quartic obstruction. Hence class $2$ is absent. A <subgroup> of $\{1,2,p,2p\}$ omitting $2$ has order at most two, so $2^R\le2$ and $R\le1$.

For $p=5$, direct substitution verifies the nonintegral point
$$
\boxed{(x,y)=\left(\frac{25}{4},\frac{75}{8}\right).}
$$
A rational torsion point would have integral coordinates by the preceding theorem, so this point has infinite order. Thus $R\ge1$, and together with the upper bound,
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{rank}E_5(\mathbb Q)=1.}
$$
